WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:05.560
A practical system hardening routine starts with inventory, ownership, and classification.

00:00:05.780 --> 00:00:12.180
Know the workstations, laptops, servers, cloud instances, containers, appliances,

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:16.000
administrative systems, and unmanaged devices in scope.

00:00:16.220 --> 00:00:21.580
Identify the owner, business purpose, criticality, data sensitivity, exposure,

00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:23.720
and support status.

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:26.020
That context drives the rest of the work.

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:29.760
Apply tested baselines and reduce unnecessary exposure.

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:33.340
Use realistic configuration standards for each system role.

00:00:33.560 --> 00:00:39.000
Remove unused software, disable unnecessary services, restrict remote access,

00:00:39.220 --> 00:00:42.420
review local accounts, and document exceptions.

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:46.000
Keep required functions reliable while reducing avoidable risk.

00:00:46.220 --> 00:00:49.420
Hardening should make systems easier to understand and defend,

00:00:49.640 --> 00:00:51.320
not harder to operate safely.

00:00:51.540 --> 00:00:54.180
Control privilege and maintain software.

00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:58.320
Use standard user access by default, limit administrative rights,

00:00:58.540 --> 00:01:03.180
review privileged groups, manage service accounts, protect emergency access,

00:01:03.400 --> 00:01:06.280
patch operating systems and third-party applications,

00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:11.380
replace unsupported software, and keep endpoint protection enabled and monitored.

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:16.640
Connect patching decisions to inventory, classification, and change control.

00:01:16.860 --> 00:01:20.540
Finally, monitor, recover, validate, and improve.

00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:25.960
Collect useful logs, review endpoint alerts, track backup status, test recovery,

00:01:26.180 --> 00:01:31.060
validate baseline application, manage change windows, preserve rollback plans,

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:32.960
and review exceptions.

00:01:33.180 --> 00:01:38.220
System hardening is practical risk reduction through disciplined operations: inventory,

00:01:38.440 --> 00:01:45.800
classify, baseline, reduce exposure, control privilege, patch, monitor, validate,

00:01:46.020 --> 00:01:47.060
and improve.
